<blockquote><p>
One might conjecture that there is an interesting fact concerning each of the positive integers. Here is a &#8216;proof by induction&#8217; that such is the case. Certainly, 1, which is a factor of each positive integer, qualifies, as do 2, the smallest prime; 3, the smallest odd prime; 4, Bieberbach&#8217;s number; <em>etc.</em> Suppose the set <em>S</em> of positive integers concerning each of which there is no interesting fact is not vacuous, and let <em>k</em> be the smallest member of <em>S</em>. But this is a most interesting fact concerning <em>k</em>! Hence <em>S</em> has no smallest member and therefore is vacuous. Is the proof valid?
</p></blockquote>
<p>&#8212; Edwin F. Beckenbach, &#8220;Interesting Integers,&#8221; <em>American Mathematical Monthly</em>, April 1945</p>
